vance.\n   201\t`:transport-error` means it returned `cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, or\n   202\t`transport-error` — a binding that retried a dead cursor forever would spin, so\n   203\tthe terminal read outcomes surface as their own status, and the status is\n   204\tretained: later `advance` calls return `:transport-error` without reading\n   205\tagain, until `recover-input-gap` re-establishes the input lane. `:closed` means teardown\n   274\t\n   275\t(deftest a-late-binding-observes-the-retained-suffix-silently\n   276\t  ;; the origin-cursor guarantee is a timing discipline: a binding whose\n   277\t  ;; first advance happens after production and eviction has already\n   278\t  ;; occurred mints oldest at the earliest retained position and observes\n   279\t  ;; the suffix with no gap — nothing is wrong with that cursor\n   280\t  (let [input (ring 1)\n   281\t        _ (ds/append! input\n   282\t                      (u/rt 0 u/t0 :subscription (u/sub-add \"s0\" ::node :tap)))\n   283\t        _ (ds/append! input\n   284\t                      (u/rt 1 u/t0 :subscription (u/sub-add \"s1\" ::node :tap)))\n   285\t        binding (event/bind {:inputs {:runtime-input input},\n   286\t                             :outputs (streams 64)})\n   287\t        first-result (event/advance binding)\n   288\t        [status _n final] (drive (:binding first-result))]\n   289\t    (is (= :advanced (:status first-result))\n   290\t        \"no cursor exists to compare against yet, so no gap is reportable\")\n   291\t    (is (= :blocked status))\n   292\t    ;; only the retained s1 was observed; the evicted s0 was missed\n   293\t    ;; silently — pinning why the guarantee needs a pre-production cursor\n   294\t    (is (= [\"s1\"] (get-in final [:state :subscription-order])))))\n   295\t\n   296\t\n   297\t(deftest recover-input-gap-resumes-at-the-next-retained-input\n   298\t  (let [input (ring 2)\n   299\t        origin (oldest input)\n   300\t        _ (doseq [i (range 4)]\n47:  freshly minted oldest cursor. A gap adopts the recovery cursor and keeps\n240:          ;; the origin cursor is minted before any value exists, so the\n278:  ;; occurred mints oldest at the earliest retained position and observes\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"**The three P1 fixes are confirmed by static review. The P2 documentation fix is nearly complete. Verdict: not yet ready for final Architect sign-off, because the manual smoke checks remain outstanding and the origin documentation still contains a contradictory claim.**\n\n1. **Initial-frame ordering — fixed.**  \n   Flutter initializes the terminal binding, invokes `:on-bind`, then starts the ticker. These managed resources use `:refresh-on :never`, so the hook runs once per mount. The dao.gui hook emits a fresh sample after binding, including when reopening with unchanged dimensions.\n\n   One correction to the implementation summary: the parent `LayoutBuilder` still emits on dimension changes. The fix adds a guaranteed post-bind emission; it does not remove every pre-bind emission. That closes the identified ordering gap. The new terminal test checks rebinding semantics, but does not exercise Flutter’s actual mount lifecycle.\n\n2. **Protocol vocabulary and frame identity — fixed.**  \n   The canonical spec, terminal document, implementation, and tests now agree on `:dao.terminal/transport-error`, with the raw stream outcome separate. All four specified terminal read outcomes reach that signal. `:presented-frame-id` starts at `nil`, advances only after successful presentation, and survives rejection/skipping unchanged. Tests cover no prior presentation and divergence between submission and presented-frame counters. **The vocabulary question no longer blocks sign-off.**\n\n3. **Repeated reads after transport failure — fixed, including mint failure.**  \n   `advance` retains `:input-error?` for terminal read outcomes and checks it before either cursor minting or reading again. Non-`closed` mint failures also retain the flag; `closed` retains the intended `:end` behavior. Explicit recovery clears the flag. The revised test meaningfully asserts that the second advance leaves the read count at one. Mint-failure handling is confirmed by inspection, rather than a dedicated regression test.\n\n4. **Origin-cursor timing — behavior pinned, documentation cleanup remains.**  \n   The new timing paragraph is accurate, and the capacity-one test verifies that late cursor minting silently observes only the retained suffix.\n\n   However, [dao.gui.event.md:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.gui.event.md:179) still says creating the binding before input exists guarantees origin observation. The `bind` docstring repeats that claim. Remove or qualify both statements to match the new paragraph.\n\n   Also, the test constructs the binding **after** both appends. It pins late minting, but does not reproduce the exact earlier counterexample—binding first, then two appends, then first advance. Moving binding construction before the appends would cover that distinction directly.\n\nI accepted the reported three-host suite results and did not rerun them. `git diff --check` passed. No files were edited. Flutter/browser animation, pause, initial display/reopening, drag, and keyboard smoke checks remain unverified; passing suites do not close that acceptance gap."}}
